<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Petra</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Raqmu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Petra is an ancient city in southern Jordan, set in sandstone mountains near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabah">Wadi Araba</a>. It was the capital of the Nabataeans, who grew rich from the caravan trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and carved tombs and temples into its rose-coloured cliffs. Annexed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 106 CE, it declined after earthquakes and shifts in trade, and it has been a World Heritage Site since 1985.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/petra.jpg" alt="Petra" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Azurfrog · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Deir_Petra.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
